/*
	RMSTEST 19 — does #undefine do anything, and does it eat its argument?

	Two questions, both about a directive the guide lists under Non-Functional
	Syntax. That appendix was built by string-searching the game's own
	non-localized-key-value-strings file, and it reports that none of the
	strings it found appear to do anything. That is a NEGATIVE claim from a
	string dump, hedged in the guide's own wording, and it has never been put
	in front of the engine.

	It now carries weight it did not carry before. validate()'s guard analysis
	(parser-design Sec.8, RMS0301/RMS0314) skips the monotonicity check for
	NEGATED conditions on the grounds that definitions in RMS only ever
	accumulate — a name undefined at a later line must have been undefined at
	an earlier one. That shortcut is sound only while #undefine is inert. If it
	works, the analysis can call a live assignment dead. RMS0310 also tells the
	user outright that deleting an #undefine line changes nothing.

	ONE MAP, BOTH ANSWERS. The two observables are independent — object counts
	for Q1, the colour of the map for Q2 — so neither can mask the other.

	--------------------------------------------------------------------
	Q1. DOES #undefine REMOVE A FLAG?

	  #define TEST_FLAG then #undefine TEST_FLAG, then branch on it.

	  7 BOAR present  -> #undefine did NOTHING. The guide is right, the
	                     shortcut in validate() is sound, nothing changes.
	  7 BOAR absent   -> #undefine WORKS. The guide's appendix is wrong about
	                     this entry. subsumes() in src/parser/validate.ts must
	                     then check negated guards for an intervening #undefine
	                     exactly as it checks positive ones for a #define, and
	                     RMS0310's message needs rewriting.

	  CONTROL: 5 RELIC, behind a flag that is defined and never undefined.
	  If the relics are missing the map itself is broken and Q1 says nothing —
	  a conditional that never fires would otherwise look exactly like a
	  working #undefine. (RMSTEST 7 shipped without a control and its result
	  was unreadable; RMSTEST 9 had to re-run it.)

	--------------------------------------------------------------------
	Q2. DOES #undefine CONSUME THE FOLLOWING TOKEN?

	  Unanswerable from the guide, which lists the bare word and no arity. It
	  decides whether `#undefine FOO` is one statement or two, and therefore
	  whether language.json's one-argument entry for it is right at all.

	  The probe is `#undefine base_terrain` followed by `SNOW` on its own line.

	  Map is WHITE       -> the operand was NOT consumed. `base_terrain` stayed
	                        in the stream as a command and took SNOW as its
	                        argument. `#undefine FOO` is two statements, and
	                        language.json should carry NO arguments for it.
	  Map is NOT white   -> the operand WAS consumed. `base_terrain` was eaten
	                        by the directive and SNOW was left as a stray word
	                        the engine ignored. The current one-argument entry
	                        is right.

	  Reading this needs no probe and no counting — it is the whole backdrop
	  of the map. Player lands paint themselves GRASS, so judge the colour
	  BETWEEN the lands, not on them.

	--------------------------------------------------------------------
	WHAT AGE OF RMS PREDICTS TODAY, recorded before the run so the comparison
	is honest rather than reconstructed afterwards:

	  Q1 — it keeps TEST_FLAG defined (parser-design Sec.7, on the guide's
	       word). It predicts 7 BOAR.
	  Q2 — its argument consumption stops at known command and attribute
	       names, so `base_terrain` is NOT eaten and the pair parses as
	       `base_terrain SNOW`. It predicts a WHITE map. Note this is a
	       side-effect of an error-tolerance rule, not a considered claim
	       about the engine, which is exactly why it is worth testing.

	  It also emits an info-level RMS0201 on line 91 — "#undefine expects 1
	  argument but only 0 were found" — which is the arity assumption in
	  language.json surfacing as a false complaint about a legal line. If Q2
	  comes back WHITE, that entry's `arguments` array should be deleted
	  outright rather than left unverified.

	--------------------------------------------------------------------
	READ IT WITH THE PROBE (tools/scenario-probe) if exporting:
	  RELIC count  -> expect 5, always. Anything else invalidates the run.
	  BOAR count   -> 7 or 0. That is Q1.
	  terrain histogram -> a large SNOW (32) count is Q2's "white".

	DESIGN NOTES
	  No placement constraints on either object. Constraints can silently fail
	  to place, and a short count would be indistinguishable from a branch that
	  did not run — the same failure mode the control exists to rule out.
	  Counts are 5 and 7 so a miscount still cannot confuse them.
	  Neither object spawns on its own, so every one on the map came from here.
	  base_terrain is deliberately NOT set anywhere else; Q2 owns it.

	RUN ON: any size, 2 players. Nothing here is size-dependent.
*/

#define CONTROL_FLAG

#define TEST_FLAG
#undefine TEST_FLAG

<PLAYER_SETUP>
random_placement

<LAND_GENERATION>

/* Q2's probe. Do not add a base_terrain line anywhere else in this file. */
#undefine base_terrain
SNOW

create_player_lands
{
	terrain_type GRASS
	base_size 6
	number_of_tiles 1
	circle_radius 40 0
}

<OBJECTS_GENERATION>

/* Control: proves a conditional block places objects at all. */
if CONTROL_FLAG
create_object RELIC
{
	number_of_objects 5
	set_gaia_object_only
}
endif

/* Q1: this flag was #define'd and then #undefine'd. */
if TEST_FLAG
create_object BOAR
{
	number_of_objects 7
	set_gaia_object_only
}
endif
